<article class="prose">

<h2 id="before-you-start">Before You Start</h2>

<p>A few things worth knowing before diving in:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Your role at home is to build consistent practice and routine — not to replace your child's Tajweed teacher. The two work together.</li>
  <li>You don't need to know Tajweed rules in technical depth yourself. You're providing structure and encouragement; correction of subtle errors is the teacher's job.</li>
  <li>A short daily habit beats a long, occasional one. This matters more than almost anything else in this guide.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="the-steps">The Step-by-Step Approach</h2>

<div class="step-card">
  <div class="step-num">1</div>
  <div>
    <h3>Start with correct letter pronunciation</h3>
    <p>Before introducing any rules, make sure your child can pronounce each Arabic letter clearly and from the right place in the mouth or throat. This is called Makharij. Rushing past this stage is the single most common reason children develop pronunciation habits that are hard to unlearn later — for example, blurring <span dir="rtl" lang="ar">ح</span> and <span dir="rtl" lang="ar">هـ</span> into the same sound.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step-card">
  <div class="step-num">2</div>
  <div>
    <h3>Introduce one rule at a time, in plain language</h3>
    <p>Rather than explaining Tajweed terminology upfront, describe what a rule sounds like first. For example: "when you see this mark and this letter comes next, hold the 'n' sound in your nose for a moment" is easier for a child to grasp than starting with the term Ikhfa. Attach the correct term once the sound itself feels natural.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step-card">
  <div class="step-num">3</div>
  <div>
    <h3>Practice new rules in short, familiar Surahs</h3>
    <p>Al-Fatiha, Al-Ikhlas, Al-Falaq, and An-Nas are commonly used because they're short, familiar, and contain a good range of common rules. Practicing a new rule in a Surah your child already knows by heart lets them focus entirely on pronunciation rather than also trying to recall the words.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step-card">
  <div class="step-num">4</div>
  <div>
    <h3>Build a short, consistent daily habit</h3>
    <p>Five to ten focused minutes a day tends to produce steadier progress than an hour once a week, especially for younger children whose attention naturally wanders. Same time each day, same short routine, low pressure.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step-card">
  <div class="step-num">5</div>
  <div>
    <h3>Get feedback beyond your own ear</h3>
    <p>Even attentive parents can miss subtle pronunciation errors, especially ones they didn't grow up correcting themselves. Two things help here: regular check-ins with your child's actual teacher, and a consistent way to catch mistakes during daily practice at home. This is where a tool like <a href="https://qariai.app">QariAI</a> fits in — it listens to daily practice and flags specific rules that need work, so you have something concrete to bring back to the teacher.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout honest">
  <div class="callout-label">Being Clear About Limits</div>
  <p>Tools like QariAI are genuinely useful for catching what might otherwise go unnoticed between lessons — but they are not validated specifically on children's voices yet, and they don't replace a teacher's correction or the chain of transmission a qualified Qari provides. Use them as a supplement to real instruction, ideally with a parent present, not as a stand-in for either.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common Mistakes Parents Make</h2>

<ul class="mistake-list">
  <li><strong>Correcting memorization speed before pronunciation is solid.</strong> A child who has memorized a Surah quickly but pronounces several letters incorrectly hasn't actually learned it well — the mistakes just get repeated faster.</li>
  <li><strong>Comparing progress to other children.</strong> Pace varies enormously by child. Comparisons tend to create anxiety rather than motivation.</li>
  <li><strong>Long, infrequent sessions instead of short daily ones.</strong> An hour once a week is much less effective than ten minutes daily, particularly for building lasting habits.</li>
  <li><strong>Only correcting mistakes, never acknowledging progress.</strong> Tajweed practice can start to feel like a string of corrections. Naming what's improved matters as much as flagging what still needs work.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="when-to-get-help">When to Bring In Additional Support</h2>

<p>If your child has been practicing consistently for a few months and specific mistakes aren't improving, or if you genuinely aren't sure what correct pronunciation should sound like for a particular letter or rule, that's a sign to involve a qualified teacher more closely rather than continuing to self-correct at home. A teacher with an ijazah can identify things that are difficult to self-diagnose, and that correction matters more than any tool.</p>

